    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born in 1861 into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> family at the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> renaissance, Tagore wrote his first poems as a child, managed the family estates in rural Bengal and drew on that life in his short stories, poems and songs.</li><li>He translated his Gitanjali into English in 1912 and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1913, the first Indian and first non-European to do so, then founded Visva-Bharati as a school and university meant to link <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the world.</li><li>A critic of nationalism who also denounced British rule, he renounced his knighthood after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jallianwala-bagh-massacre">Jallianwala Bagh massacre</a> of 1919, and he disagreed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> on several matters, while some critics judged his English translations weak.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>At sixteen Tagore published poems under the pseudonym Bhanusimha, claiming they were the lost works of a newly discovered 17th-century Vaishnava poet, and regional experts accepted them as such.</p></aside>
    </section>

