    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1861</span><span class="rf-text">Born in the Jorasanko mansion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, the youngest of 13 surviving children</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1877</span><span class="rf-text">Presents poems as the lost works of a 17th-century poet, Bhanusimha, and debuts in the short story with &quot;Bhikharini&quot;</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1890</span><span class="rf-text">Begins managing the family estates at Shelaidaha and publishes the Manasi poems</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1901</span><span class="rf-text">Moves to Santiniketan to found an ashram with an experimental school</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1913</span><span class="rf-text">Learns in November that he has won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1919</span><span class="rf-text">Renounces his knighthood after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jallianwala-bagh-massacre">Jallianwala Bagh massacre</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1921</span><span class="rf-text">Visva-Bharati is inaugurated three years after its foundation stone was laid</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1941</span><span class="rf-text">Dies at Jorasanko on 7 August, aged 80</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Rabindranath Tagore, nicknamed Rabi, was born on 7 May 1861 in the Jorasanko mansion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, the youngest of 13 surviving children of Debendranath Tagore (1817–1905) and Sarada Devi (1830–1875). The name Tagore is an anglicised form of Thakur; the family were Pirali <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, a group that historically carried a stigmatised connotation. His mother died in his early childhood and his father travelled widely, so servants raised him. The household was at the forefront of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> renaissance, hosting literary magazines, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and recitals of Bengali and Western classical music, and Debendranath invited Dhrupad musicians to teach the children. His brothers included the philosopher and poet Dwijendranath, Satyendranath, the first Indian appointed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-civil-service">Indian Civil Service</a>, and the musician and playwright Jyotirindranath.</p><p>Tagore largely avoided classroom schooling and preferred to roam the manor or nearby Bolpur and Panihati. His brother Hemendranath tutored him and made him swim the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, trek and wrestle, and he learned drawing, anatomy, geography, history, literature, mathematics, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and English, his least favourite subject. His time at Presidency College lasted a single day. After his upanayan at eleven he toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> with his father from February 1873, visiting Santiniketan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a> before reaching the hill station of Dalhousie, where he read biographies and studied history, astronomy, science and Sanskrit.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Rabindranath Tagore&#8221; cites where it covers Family background, Early life: 1861–1878:</p><ul><li>R. Som, Rabindranath Tagore: The Singer and His Song (Viking, 2010)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By 1877 Tagore had completed a set of major works, among them a long poem in the Maithili style of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidyapati">Vidyapati</a>. As a joke he claimed they were the lost works of a newly discovered 17th-century Vaishnava poet, Bhanusimha, and regional experts accepted them. That year he also debuted in the short-story genre with "Bhikharini", and the collections Sandhya Sangit (1882) and Prabhat Sangit (1883) followed. His father wanted him to become a barrister, so in 1878 he enrolled at a public school in Brighton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and briefly read law at University College <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> before leaving to study Shakespeare and Thomas Browne on his own. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in 1880 without a degree.</p><p>In 1883 he married Mrinalini Devi, then nine years old, a common practice at the time; they had five children, two of whom died in childhood. The suicide in 1884 of Kadambari Devi, his brother Jyotirindranath's wife and a close friend, left him distraught for years. After his return his poems, stories and novels had a profound influence in Bengal but received little national attention. In 1890 he began managing the family estates at Shelaidaha, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>, where he published the Manasi poems and travelled the Padma River on the family barge.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/w-b-yeats"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/w-b-yeats.jpg" alt="W. B. Yeats" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/w-b-yeats">W. B. Yeats</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Butler_Yeats_by_George_Charles_Beresford.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">George Charles Beresford, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The years 1891 to 1895, called his Sadhana period after one of his magazines, were his most productive. In them he wrote more than half the stories of the 84-story Galpaguchchha, among them "Kabuliwala" and "Kshudita Pashan", drawing on what he saw of poor villagers while managing the estates. He met Gagan Harkara, through whom he came to know the Baul Lalon Shah, whose folk songs influenced him and which he worked to popularise. In 1901 he moved to Santiniketan to found an ashram with a prayer hall, an experimental school, gardens and a library.</p><p>He translated his 1910 Gitanjali into English in 1912 and shared it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> with admirers including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-b-yeats">William Butler Yeats</a> and Ezra Pound; in November 1913 he learned that he had won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-academy">Swedish Academy</a> appreciated the idealistic and, for Westerners, accessible character of a small body of his translated work centred on Gitanjali: Song Offerings. The foundation stone of Visva-Bharati was laid on 24 December 1918 and it was inaugurated three years later, with teaching often under trees; Tagore contributed his Nobel prize money and wrote the students' textbooks. In 1921, with the agricultural economist Leonard Elmhirst, he set up the Institute for Rural Reconstruction, later Shriniketan. His songs, about 2,230 in all, include "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jana-gana-mana">Jana Gana Mana</a>", first sung in 1911 and adopted as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s national anthem in 1950, and "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amar-sonar-bangla">Amar Sonar Bangla</a>", which became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>'s in 1971. At sixty he took up painting, and his works were exhibited across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Rabindranath Tagore&#8221; cites where it covers Drama, Short stories, Poetry, Songs (Rabindra Sangeet):</p><ul><li>H. B. Urban, Songs of Ecstasy: Tantric and Devotional Songs from Colonial Bengal (Oxford University Press, 2001)</li><li>Krishna Dutta, Selected Letters of Rabindranath Tagore (Cambridge University Press, 1997)</li><li>R. Som, Rabindranath Tagore: The Singer and His Song (Viking, 2010)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi.jpg" alt="Mahatma Gandhi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahatma-Gandhi,_studio,_1931.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elliott &amp; Fry, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Tagore's politics drew anger from several sides. He opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> and denounced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a>, and evidence produced in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>–German Conspiracy Trial and later accounts affirm his awareness of the Ghadarites and that he sought Japanese support, yet he also lampooned the Swadeshi movement and rebuked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s charkha cult in the 1925 essay The Cult of the Charkha. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amartya-sen">Amartya Sen</a>, he rebelled against strongly nationalist forms of the independence movement while insisting on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s right to be independent. In his view British administration was a "political symptom of our social disease", and even for the very poor "there can be no question of blind revolution"; he preferred "steady and purposeful education". His essay "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nationalism">Nationalism</a> in India" was scorned and praised. In late 1916 Indian expatriates plotted to assassinate him in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> hotel, and the plan failed when they fell into an argument.</p><p>He also criticised Gandhi directly. In 1934 he rebuked Gandhi for calling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> earthquake divine retribution for the oppression of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalit">Dalits</a>. After a warm first meeting with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> in 1926 he felt compelled to denounce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascism">Fascism</a> in The Manchester Guardian. His work met mixed judgements: Graham Greene doubted that anyone but Yeats could take his poems seriously, and Yeats himself wrote that Tagore had published "sentimental rubbish" and "wrecked his reputation". <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/e-m-forster">E. M. Forster</a> found that The Home and the World had lost its charms in translation. His novels remain among the least appreciated of his works.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Dutta and Robinson describe his last years as those of a "peripatetic litterateur". Between 1878 and 1932 he had set foot in more than thirty countries on five continents. In 1932 he visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, and in 1933 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, in what was his final foreign tour. In Iraq a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> chief quoted a saying of the Prophet to him, and Tagore wrote in his diary that he was "startled into recognising in his words the voice of essential humanity". His dislike of communalism and nationalism deepened. In 1937 his paintings were removed by the Nazi regime from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> palace, and five were included in the inventory of "degenerate art" compiled in 1941–1942.</p><p>In these years fifteen new volumes appeared, among them the prose-poem works Punashcha (1932), Shes Saptak (1935) and Patraput (1936), and the dance-dramas Chandalika (1938) and Shyama (1939). His interest turned to science, as in the essays of Visva-Parichay (1937) and the stories of Se (1937) and Galpasalpa (1941). His last five years were marked by chronic pain and two long illnesses, which began when he lost consciousness in late 1937 and again in late 1940, from the second of which he never recovered. He died on 7 August 1941, aged 80, in an upstairs room of the Jorasanko mansion where he was raised. His last poem was dictated on 30 July to A. K. Sen, a day before a scheduled operation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/amartya-sen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amartya-sen.jpg" alt="Amartya Sen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/amartya-sen">Amartya Sen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amartya_Sen_20071128_cologne_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elke Wetzig (Elya); derivative work by ChaudhryAzan, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Tagore's compositions were chosen by two nations as national anthems, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>'s anthem was inspired by his work; his song "Banglar Mati Banglar Jol" is the state anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amartya-sen">Amartya Sen</a> called him a "towering figure" and a "deeply relevant and many-sided contemporary thinker". His songs are central to Bengali life: the Modern Review observed that there was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> "no cultured home where Rabindranath's songs are not sung or at least attempted to be sung". Eight museums commemorate him, three in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and five in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>, and Jorasanko Thakur Bari, his birthplace, is now part of the Rabindra Bharati University campus.</p><p>Abroad his reception varied. Through translations he influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-neruda">Pablo Neruda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriela-mistral">Gabriela Mistral</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/octavio-paz">Octavio Paz</a> and Juan Ramón Jiménez, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> the Nobel laureate Yasunari Kawabata. He co-founded Dartington Hall School. His lecture tours in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1916–1917 were widely attended, but controversies, possibly fictive, damaged his popularity and sales in Japan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> after the late 1920s, concluding with his "near total eclipse" outside Bengal. William Radice asked what place his poems hold in world literature, and many readers hold that the translations never matched the Bengali originals. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> was stolen from the Visva-Bharati vault in March 2004, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-academy">Swedish Academy</a> presented two replicas that December.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Rabindranath Tagore (1861–1941), born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, wrote poems, stories, novels, plays and about 2,230 songs in Bengali, won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1913 and founded Visva-Bharati. He wrote the words of the anthems of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>, criticised nationalism and British rule alike, and renounced his knighthood in 1919.</em></p>

